          <!-- Section: What Are The Cu Chi Tunnels? -->
          <section id="what-are-tunnels" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                What Are The Cu Chi Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              The Cu Chi Tunnels aren't a single cavern complex. They are the surviving remnants of an underground fortification network built across the rural district of Cu Chi, roughly 50 to 70 kilometers northwest of downtown Ho Chi Minh City.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              The network didn't start as a massive 250-kilometer underground city. Following the outbreak of the French resistance in southern Vietnam, local villagers and militia began digging simple individual hiding pits (<em>hầm bí mật</em>) starting around 1946. Around 1948, these isolated shelters in Tan Phu Trung and Phuoc Vinh An communes were interconnected into the first continuous subterranean passages of about 17 km. Between 1961 and 1965, facing escalating aerial bombardment and ground sweeps across the "Iron Triangle," local forces expanded the network into a fortified, three-tiered defensive labyrinth linking entire communes.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Understanding that evolution matters for visitors today.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              When you arrive at Cu Chi, you are not stepping into an endless underground maze. Most of the original wartime network collapsed or was reclaimed by farmland and jungle after 1975. Today, roughly 4 km of passages are conserved and maintained across the two main visitor sectors at Ben Duoc and Ben Dinh, with additional historical traces surviving at Xom Bung, An Binh, and Cay Da.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              The preserved visitor sites show how subterranean spaces fulfilled everyday survival functions:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-[15.5px]">
              <li><strong>Concealed Movement:</strong> Shifting troops and supplies beneath artillery barrages without being spotted from above.</li>
              <li><strong>Ambush Points:</strong> Camouflaged trapdoors letting fighters strike quickly and vanish back into the earth.</li>
              <li><strong>Living Quarters:</strong> Sleeping platforms with hammock hooks, storage recesses, and fresh air ducts.</li>
              <li><strong>Medical Bunkers:</strong> Operating rooms lit by modified kerosene lamps where doctors performed surgery on wounded soldiers.</li>
              <li><strong>Ordnance Workshops:</strong> Reclaiming unexploded American bombs and shells to forge punji traps, mines, and grenades.</li>
              <li><strong>Command Posts:</strong> Meeting chambers for regional military commanders and district leadership committees.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Life Underground Was More Than Warfare -->
          <section id="life-underground" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Life Underground Was More Than Warfare
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Life below ground wasn't just firefights and combat. For weeks at a time, soldiers, medics, and local civilians lived entirely beneath the surface.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              UNESCO's 2026 nomination dossier highlights spaces dedicated to resting, community meetings, grain storage, hand-dug wells, medical care, and even makeshift theatrical performances. Children attended basic lessons under the glow of oil lamps, cobblers cut tire sandals from downed aircraft wreckage, and cultural troupes sang folk songs to maintain morale during weeks of intense bombing. That is why Vietnamese historians refer to Cu Chi as an underground combat village (<em>làng hầm</em>) rather than simply a military trench.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: History & Multi-Tier Architecture -->
          <section id="history-engineering" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                A Brief History & Multi-Tier Architecture
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Digging through Cu Chi's soil was grueling manual labor. Guerrillas worked mostly at night using short-handled triangular hoes (<em>cuốc</em>) and woven bamboo baskets, dumping excavated dirt into fresh bomb craters or into the Saigon River to avoid detection from reconnaissance planes.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              The local soil—an iron-rich, reddish-brown laterite clay—turned out to be an ideal engineering material. When freshly dug, it is pliable enough to carve by hand. But once exposed to air and dried, laterite cures to near-brick density. Carved with vaulted, curved ceilings, these tunnels withstood heavy aerial bombardment and the weight of passing tanks without collapsing.
            </p>
            
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 my-4 space-y-3">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base">Between 1965 and 1968, the network evolved into three distinct depth tiers:</div>
              <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
                <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                  <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</span>
                  <div><strong>First Tier (approx. 3 meters deep):</strong> Built with firing slits, communication trenches, and zigzag corners designed to break the shockwaves of exploding artillery shells.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                  <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</span>
                  <div><strong>Second Tier (approx. 5 to 6 meters deep):</strong> Housed living spaces, kitchens, supply depots, meeting rooms, and field hospitals.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                  <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</span>
                  <div><strong>Third Tier (approx. 8 to 10+ meters deep):</strong> The deepest sanctuary, containing emergency command shelters, fresh water wells, and secret escape tunnels running toward the Saigon River. At Ben Duoc, an original 20-meter tunnel section still leads toward the riverbank today.</div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: How The Network Worked -->
          <section id="how-network-worked" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                How The Underground Network Functioned
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              To appreciate Cu Chi, you have to understand the architectural ingenuity that made subterranean life viable for months at a time:
            </p>

            <div class="my-6 not-prose">
              <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-md border border-slate-200">
                <img 
                  src="https://media.thericetour.com/uploads/guide-explaining-cu-chi-tunnel-cross-section-model-diorama.webp" 
                  alt="Licensed tour guide explaining the three-tiered underground network using a detailed cross-section model diorama at Cu Chi" 
                  class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]"
                  width="1280"
                  height="720"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-500 italic mt-2.5">
                Licensed tour guide using a detailed cross-section model diorama to illustrate the multi-tiered tunnel architecture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
              <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-2">
                <div class="text-2xl mb-1">🚪</div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base">1. Concealed Entrances (<em>Cửa Hầm Bí Mật</em>)</h3>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  Surface entry shafts were cut just wide enough for a slender human frame—about 25 by 40 cm. Wooden trapdoor lids were plastered with clay and scattered with dry forest leaves. On-site guides still demonstrate how a person could drop feet-first into the shaft, pull the lid flush with the forest floor, and vanish in under three seconds.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-2">
                <div class="text-2xl mb-1">🐜</div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base">2. Disguised Air Vents</h3>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  People living underground needed fresh air without giving away their position. Air shafts were drilled sideways through clay seams, venting out through hollow bamboo tubes hidden inside natural termite mounds (<em>ụ mối</em>) or rotten tree stumps, angled downward so monsoon downpours couldn't flood the chambers below.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-2">
                <div class="text-2xl mb-1">🍳</div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base">3. The Smokeless Hoàng Cầm Stove (<em>Bếp Hoàng Cầm</em>)</h3>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  Cooking underground was dangerous because rising smoke drew immediate airstrikes. In late 1951, military cook Hoàng Cầm invented a stove with underground exhaust flues. Smoke was channeled through long, gently sloping trenches covered by branches and tamped earth. By the time it traveled 20 meters to the surface, the smoke had cooled and dispersed along the ground like early morning forest mist.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-2">
                <div class="text-2xl mb-1">💧</div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base">4. Fresh Water & River Exits</h3>
                <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
                  Hand-dug wells reached down to clean groundwater veins on the third tier. Hidden underwater exits also opened below the waterline of the Saigon River, letting couriers and scouts swim out into river currents unseen by riverbank patrols.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="my-6 not-prose">
              <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-md border border-slate-200">
                <img 
                  src="https://media.thericetour.com/uploads/man-holding-leaf-covered-tunnel-cover-secret-entrance-cu-chi.webp" 
                  alt="A demonstrator holds open the wooden leaf-covered trapdoor hatch, illustrating how it seals flush with the earthen forest floor" 
                  class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]"
                  width="1280"
                  height="720"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="text-center text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-500 italic mt-2.5">
                Demonstration of the wooden, leaf-camouflaged trapdoor cover engineered to seal flush with the forest floor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Are The Visitor Tunnels Original? -->
          <section id="original-vs-adapted" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Are The Cu Chi Tunnels You Crawl Through Original?
              </h2>
            </div>
            
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50/70 border border-amber-200 text-slate-800 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed my-4">
              <div class="font-bold text-amber-900 mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span>🔍</span> Authenticity & Conservation Reality
              </div>
              <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The short answer: Yes, they are authentic wartime tunnels, but they have been significantly adapted for tourism. You are not entering an untouched 1968 combat trench. Following heavy wartime damage, restoration began in 1978 and expanded throughout the 1980s and 1990s. The sections open to the public today have been reinforced with concrete collars, equipped with dim electric lights, and widened by 20% to 30% so international visitors can fit through safely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Neither visitor site should be described as an "untouched" relic. Following wartime damage, restoration and repair began as early as 1978. Through continuous conservation in the 1980s and major embellishment in 1991, approximately 4 km across Ben Duoc and Ben Dinh were stabilized to allow safe public education.
            </p>

