          <!-- Section: Do You Have To Crawl? -->
          <section id="do-you-have-to-crawl" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Do You Have To Crawl Through The Tunnels?
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] font-semibold text-emerald-800 bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 p-4 rounded-xl">
              No. Entering the subterranean tunnels is 100% voluntary.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              A common worry among first-time visitors is that visiting Cu Chi requires crawling on your knees through pitch-black dirt. That isn't how the tour works.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              In reality, <strong>over 90% of the experience takes place above ground</strong> along shaded forest trails:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-[15.5px]">
              <li>Reconstructed bunkers, surgical triage rooms, and command headquarters have open roofs or wide stairwells with safety handrails.</li>
              <li>Trapdoor demonstrations, booby-trap exhibitions, and diorama models are all viewed from ground level.</li>
              <li>You can watch the documentary, inspect the tank, examine the bomb craters, and eat the cassava without ever ducking underground.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              If anyone in your group has bad knees, back issues, heart conditions, or claustrophobia, they can relax comfortably under shaded pavilions while others try the crawl.
            </p>

            <div class="my-6 not-prose">
              <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-md border border-slate-200">
