              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Crawling Realities & Body Size Check -->
          <section id="crawling-feel-realities" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                What Crawling Actually Feels Like & Body Size Reality
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              If you do choose to enter the tunnel, knowing what to expect physically helps avoid panic:
            </p>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3.5 my-4">
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">Dimensions & Posture</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  Even in widened tourist sections (~80 cm wide by 120 cm high), adults cannot stand upright. You must crouch, duck-waddle, or crawl on hands and knees.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">Heat & Atmosphere</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  It's warm (typically 32°C to 36°C) and humid. The air smells intensely of dry soil and clay. Low-voltage bulbs provide dim yellow light every few meters.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">Physical Exertion</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  Duck-walking burns your thighs within thirty seconds. A 20-meter crawl takes only two minutes, but you will emerge sweating and breathing hard.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">Emergency Exits</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  Passages have exit ladders every 15 to 20 meters. You don't have to commit to a long crawl—try the first 15 meters, and if your knees ache or the close space bothers you, climb right out.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Body Size Reality Check Callout -->
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50/70 border border-amber-200 text-slate-800 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed my-4">
              <div class="font-bold text-amber-900 mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2">
                <span>📏</span> Is The Tunnel Suitable For Tall Or Larger Travelers?
              </div>
              <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tunnel sections open to visitors have been enlarged compared with original wartime passages, but they can still feel remarkably tight for tall or broader travelers. If you are over 6 ft (183 cm), have broad shoulders, or dislike crouching in confined spaces, test the first 15-meter stretch before deciding whether to continue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-100 border border-slate-200 text-slate-700 text-sm">
              <strong>💡 Practical Rule:</strong> If you suffer from acute claustrophobia, asthma, high blood pressure, or joint pain, step into the entrance shaft for a photo, then climb straight back out. Nobody will judge you for skipping the crawl.
            </div>
          </section>

