          <!-- Section: Why Traveler Experiences Differ -->
          <section id="why-experiences-differ" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Why Traveler Experiences At Cu Chi Differ So Much
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Visitor reviews of Cu Chi range from "one of the most moving historical sites in Southeast Asia" to "crowded, rushed, and commercialized." That difference almost always comes down to three things:
            </p>
            <div class="space-y-3 my-4">
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">1. Group Size & Arrival Timing</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  Arriving at Ben Dinh at 10:15 AM puts you right behind twenty 45-seat tour coaches. You'll wait in line for the trapdoor photo op, squeeze into crowded briefing pavilions, and move at a herd pace. Leaving Saigon at 07:30 AM in a private car lets you explore empty trails in the cool morning air.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">2. The Guide's Perspective</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  A dirt crater or a rusty bomb casing is just metal and earth without context. A knowledgeable guide shares personal family history, explains how civilian families survived without sunlight, and answers candid questions about the war.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">3. Pacing Realities</div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">
                  A thorough visit takes <strong>2 to 2.5 hours on-site</strong>. Trying to stretch Cu Chi into a whole-day tour without combining it with Saigon landmarks just leaves you sitting around in highway traffic.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Can You Visit Without A Tour? -->
          <section id="can-you-visit-without-tour" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-4">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Can You Visit Cu Chi Without Booking A Tour?
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              <strong>Yes. Cu Chi can be visited completely on your own.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              You can hire a private driver, call a Grab taxi, or take public buses from Saigon, buy your ticket at the entrance booth, and join an on-site guide. The 135,000 VND foreign entrance fee includes a site guide stationed at the gate who walks your group through the outdoor circuit and explains the traps.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              The trade-off is <strong>convenience versus control</strong>:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-[15.5px]">
              <li>An independent visit gives you full freedom over your timetable and pacing, but you handle highway navigation, parking, and timing on your own.</li>
              <li>A private guide and driver handles door-to-door pickup, times your arrival to avoid bus surges, and gives you continuous, personalized storytelling from start to finish.</li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Visit Style Table -->
