            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                What to Wear & Practical Packing List
              </h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-[16px]">
              Cu Chi is an outdoor forested archaeological site characterized by unpaved dirt trails, laterite dust, and tropical humidity:
            </p>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-4">
              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-2">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm text-emerald-700 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                  <span>✓</span> Recommended Attire
                </div>
                <ul class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
                  <li><strong>Loose, Breathable Fabrics:</strong> Linen, lightweight cotton, or moisture-wicking athletic wear.</li>
                  <li><strong>Dark or Earth-Tone Colors:</strong> Red laterite clay leaves permanent reddish-brown stains on white clothing and pristine shoes if you crawl through the tunnels or brush against trench walls. Many travelers wrap a traditional cotton <a href="/khan-ran-nam-bo" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">khăn rằn checkered scarf</a> around their neck to absorb perspiration and protect against the sun.</li>
                  <li><strong>Sturdy Closed-Toe Shoes:</strong> Sneakers or walking shoes with good traction. Avoid flip-flops, slides, or high heels.</li>
                  <li><strong>Temple-Appropriate Clothing (Ben Duoc only):</strong> If visiting the Ben Duoc Memorial Temple, shoulders and knees must be respectfully covered.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-2">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm text-rose-700 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                  <span>✕</span> What to Avoid
                </div>
                <ul class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
                  <li>White pants, pristine designer sneakers, or delicate fabrics that will stain permanently in the clay.</li>
                  <li>High heels, flip-flops, or smooth-soled shoes with zero grip on dusty inclines.</li>
                  <li>Heavy backpacks that get wedged in narrow crawl shafts.</li>
                  <li>Tight or stiff denim jeans that restrict deep knee bends and duck-walking.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm">Essential Packing Checklist:</div>
              <ul class="space-y-1 text-slate-600 list-disc pl-4">
                <li>🧴 <strong>High-SPF Sunscreen & Insect Repellent:</strong> Dense woodland canopy harbors mosquitoes.</li>
                <li>🧢 <strong>Wide-Brim Hat or Cap:</strong> Essential for sunny clearing sections.</li>
                <li>💧 <strong>Reusable Water Bottle:</strong> Staying hydrated in tropical humidity is vital.</li>
                <li>🧻 <strong>Wet Wipes or Hand Sanitizer:</strong> Indispensable for wiping red clay off your hands and knees after tunnel crawling.</li>
                <li>💵 <strong>Cash in Vietnamese Dong (VND):</strong> Entrance tickets, drinks, and firing range bullets require cash on-site. Mobile data and card terminals can be spotty under the trees.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
